ZERO0.11 WINDOWS HOST INTEROPERABILITY BACKEND

This backend is part of canonical Zero0.11. The preserved0.9 compiler and
packages remain unchanged. Product implementation is original Zero and machine
encodings. Platform calls use the host operating system's KERNEL32 APIs;
there is no bundled C runtime, assembler, foreign compiler, shell launcher,
PowerShell, Bash, Python runtime or subprocess dependency in the product.

Source:
  windows-layout.txt       original instruction bytes and local rel32 labels
  windows-author.zero      original Zero formatter/resolver for that source data
  windows-tables.zero.part resulting mode7 templates70 and86..103
  windows-finalizer.zero.part original Zero PE/import-table finalization
  ABI.ztext                  common operation and buffer/ownership contract

The formatter itself is compiled by Zero. Its input is original encoding data,
not third-party assembly. No external assembler/linker or code generator is
used. Host integration/verification scripts only assemble source files and
orchestrate tests; they implement no compiler or runtime behavior.

Native Windows startup uses GetCommandLineW and strict UTF-8 conversion, then
our own bounded argument parser. It supports quote grouping, empty arguments,
backslashes preceding quotes, literal quotes, tabs and spaces. There may be
63 user arguments, excluding the program name. The complete UTF-8 command
line including executable spelling, separators and quoting must fit4095 bytes.
An oversized line, excess arguments or unmatched quote exits64. User indices
start at1; out-of-range argument returns0. A preserved main-frame pointer keeps
these pointers valid across Zero cooperative fork/switch contexts.

program() returns a stable borrowed UTF-8 absolute module-path buffer, bounded
to4095 bytes, or0 on error. It uses GetModuleFileNameW rather than trusting argv0.
The hosted startup allocation is16KiB, containing arguments and program-path
storage, while the existing4MiB arena/argc offsets remain unchanged.

All path operations require a valid NUL-terminated string of at most4095 bytes.
MultiByteToWideChar(CP_UTF8, MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS) rejects malformed UTF-8;
wide Windows file APIs perform filesystem operations. Native Windows path
rules and filesystem limits still apply. Ancestor links are not a sandbox.

fileopen uses CreateFileW with FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT, rejects directory
and final-reparse attributes both before opening and on the opened handle,
and closes rejected handles. Mode2 uses OPEN_ALWAYS, validates the handle,
then truncates with SetEndOfFile. Modes1/4 use CREATE_NEW and never overwrite.
Read mode shares reads; write modes request exclusive sharing. ReadFile and
WriteFile preserve partial counts and binary bytes. New file I/O operations
normalize failures to u64(-1); count must not exceed0x7fffffff. EOF returns0.
Other file primitives follow ABI.ztext. MoveFileExW uses flags0: no replacement
and no cross-filesystem COPY_ALLOWED fallback. RemoveDirectoryW only removes
empty directories, after rejecting a final reparse point.

filesync[handle] calls FlushFileBuffers on an owned writable file handle,
mapping a nonzero BOOL to0 and failure to unsigned-1. It preserves the offset
and leaves the handle open. This request does not make parent directory entries
or renames crash-durable and is not proof of physical power-loss survival.
hostwait[milliseconds] calls Sleep for0..86400000 inclusive, returning0;
larger unsigned values return-1 without a Windows call. The calling native
thread sleeps, including every Zero cooperative task scheduled on that thread.
The PE import arrays now hold25 imports plus a null thunk (208 bytes).

hostalloc uses VirtualAlloc RW; hostprotect uses VirtualProtect to RW or RX
and flushes the instruction cache after RX. No RWX protection is requested.
hostfree validates a nonzero supplied size and releases the complete original
Windows allocation using VirtualFree(base,0,MEM_RELEASE), as its API requires.
Callers own the ranges and must not pass arbitrary pointers or partial bases.

VERIFICATION

Current0.11 component evidence is in durability/{results.json,
windows-verification.json}. The focused probe contains21 Windows assertions
for flush/wait success, errors, handle/stack preservation and millisecond bounds;
the native Windows compiler still self-reproduces byte-for-byte under Wine.
The existing API/Unicode/argument/cooperative-context suite also passes.
The older reports below describe0.10 and are retained as historical evidence.

windows-verification.json records the exact combined compiler/source hashes:
-61 functional API assertions, including all file modes, Unicode paths,
 binary I/O, EOF, size, overwrite refusal, truncation and dynamic native call;
-20 invalid-pointer-value, malformed UTF-8, path-limit and allocation checks;
-4-byte Unicode paths, exact argument roundtrips,63-user-argument boundary;
-absolute Unicode program path and byte-exact Windows compiler self-rebuild;
-Ezero native Windows readfile/writefile/arguments, preserving existing files.

windows-cli-verification.json records26 native compiler CLI checks: real PE
zero.exe and ezero.exe with no command interpreter, relocation and Unicode
paths, all9 target output parities, compiler/CLI self-rebuild, high-level
examples and host files, detailed errors, output preservation and app guards.
The native CLI implementation lives in tools/compiler-cli.zero, not in Python.
installer/verification/native-wine.json records18 preparation/verification/
removal checks for the original Zero setup program. It explicitly does not
install a boot entry or claim the prepared folder is bootable.

The environment is Wine10.0 on Debian13. No native Microsoft Windows machine
run is claimed. Wine10's CreateSymbolicLinkW is a stub; the test-only original
Zero fixture records this at windows-tests/link-fixture.stderr. Wine's Z:
POSIX symlinks do not expose Windows FILE_ATTRIBUTE_REPARSE_POINT, so they
cannot establish a native Windows reparse-point rejection test. Those cases
are explicitly unverified/skipped, never reported as passing. Windows disk-
full injection and cross-volume move execution are not covered here.

Only original Windows product binaries and their own source/runtime companions
belong in a distribution. Wine, QEMU, firmware and Linux host tools stay outside
it. The external test scripts and test-only import fixture are not product
runtime components.

The Windows argument-pointer contract was also exercised through the original
cooperative-context regression: two workers,128 switches per worker, checking
argcount/argument from child frames after each return. This passed under Wine.

